<time datetime="2023-06-19">June 19, 2023</time>
<article id="CSSART" class="WebBits">
    <h2>TRY: Create a CSS rule</h2>
    <p><strong>Cascading Style Sheets</strong>, <abbr title="Cascading Style Sheets">CSS</abbr>, gives color and layout to web elements based on some chosen characteristic of that element. Without applying css, website pages will look like a text document.</p>
    <div class="cssex">
        <button class="reset">RESET</button>
        <ol class="slct">
            <li class="Selector Selector-elem">Selector</li>
            <li class="Attribute">Attribute</li>
            <li class="Value">Value</li>
            <li class="Psuedo-class">Psuedo-class</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="cssfig">
            <p class="Selector CSS">#CSS</p><pre class="CSS"> {
    </pre><p class="CSS Attribute">background-color</p><pre class="CSS">: </pre><p class="CSS Value">limegreen</p><pre class="CSS">;

    </pre><p class="CSS Selector">&</p><p class="CSS Psuedo-class">:hover</p><pre class="CSS"> {
        </pre><p class="CSS Attribute">background-color</p><pre class="CSS">: </pre><p class="CSS Value">hsl(120, 61%, 45%)</p><pre class="CSS">;
    }
}
</pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <aside id="CSS" class="exampleBlue">
        <p>The above example uses nested CSS rules, each with a single attribute. The same styles give this element the green background color.</p>
    </aside>
    <p>Style sheets can compose thousands of rules of varying complexity. Rules and/or style sheets can repeat throughout a website easily. Multiple css files can be added &lpar;or <i>not</i> added&rpar; to as part of the layout.</p>
</article>
<div class="Top-Link"><a class="Top" href="/pages/css#top">Top</a></div>
